            "target_entity_instance": "Institute of Atmospheric Physics (IAP), Chinese Academy of Sciences",
            "target_entity_type": "Institute",
            "paragraph": "Despite these challenges, recent theoretical understanding and methodological advances have shown promise in improving short-term GMST prediction. One notable advance is the development of the Statistical Ensemble Prediction System (SEPS) at the Institute of Atmospheric Physics (IAP), Chinese Academy of Sciences 15. This system has demonstrated high skill in predicting GMST two months in advance (i.e., start from November) by employing a timescale-decomposition approach to explicitly consider the contributions of multiple climate factors across different temporal scales.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Indian Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Actually, the patterns revealed in Fig. 2a and 2d closely resemble the pantropical ocean interactions pattern driven by ENSO events 19. For example, during an El Ni\u00f1o event, warming in the central-eastern equatorial Pacific, typically initiated in boreal summer or autumn, weakens the Walker circulation and shifts it eastward. This leads to anomalous subsidence and reduced oceanic transport in the eastern equatorial Indian Ocean, which induces upwelling-favorable winds, decreases surface freshwater flux into ocean, and shoals the thermocline 20-22. All the processes precondition the Indian Ocean basin for the onset and amplification of positive IOD events, leading to anomalous warming SST in the tropical western-central Indian Ocean during boreal autumn 22,23.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "tropical-subtropical Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "At the same time, the shifting of the Walker circulation and the Pacific North American pattern triggered by El Ni\u00f1o, lead to anomalously low surface pressure over the tropical-subtropical Atlantic and weakens the northeasterly over the tropical northern Atlantic (TNA) 24,25. This reduction in wind speed suppresses local latent and sensible heat losses, resulting in TNA warming, particularly between 10\u00b0N\u201320\u00b0N, where also exhibit high correlation to GMST prediction errors.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o\u2013Southern Oscillation (ENSO)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "In contrast, such strong ENSO control weakens outside the boreal autumn-winter seasons. Prediction errors started before autumn show weaker relations with ENSO (r < 0.5; Fig. S2). These months display some connections to extratropical regions\u2014such as the Arctic, Eurasia, and North America (Fig. S1)\u2014where surface temperature variations are intrinsically less predictable 29 and thus contribute little to improve GMST forecast skills.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
